Z                          Overview
Z
ZSearch is composed of two parts - the daemon, and the player.
Z
ZThe daemon does most of the work.  He waits in the wings until someone
Zconnects to one of his two sockets (/tmp/slock or internet port 525).
ZThe action of connecting to one of the sockets wakes the daemon up,
Zand when the proper information is recieved the new player is accepted
Zinto the game.  A socket other than the default 525 may be specified
Zin /etc/services or as an argument to searchd.
Z
ZThis "request to play" is generated by the player process "search".
ZIf search is invoked without an argument, it is assumed that the
Zplayer simply wishes to play on the local machine, and connects
Zthe player to the local (AF_UNIX) socket "/tmp/slock".  An argument
Zto search is considered to be a remote hostname the player wants to
Zplay on.  Search will attempt to connect to that host's search
Zdaemon and to join play in the remote game.  A port other than the
Zone specified in /etc/services may be given to search as a second
Zargument.
Z
Z
ZInstallation instructions:
Z
Z0.  If search is being installed by a non-superuser you will have to
Zmake a few changes to the procedure below.  You must change the path
Znames of the game and score file to something appropriate and also
Zchoose an internet port number which is not in the reserved range.
ZSee the variables LIBDIR, OWNER, GROUP and ROOT in the Makefile.
ZThe search daemon will have to be given this port number as an argument
Zand the users will have to give it as an argument to search itself.
Z(You could instead just change DEFAULT_IN_PORT in defines.h if you
Zare putting up search as a non-su on a local network.  All of this
Zabout port numbers is of course irrelevant if you do not use -DINET.)
Z
Z1.  Decide if you wish to have the internet feature of search.
ZThis is the feature that allows people from other machines
Zto play search without having to do a remote login, etc.
ZIf you decide that this is a bad thing, or you don't have network
Zsoftware (i.e. no network), remove the -DINET from the Makefile.
Z
Z2.  Compile all of the modules by running "make".  If you're really
Zrunning 4.2 BSD it should all work correctly.
Z
Z3.  Run "make install", which will create all the score files,
Zand move everything to its proper location.  The daemon must have
Zread and write access to the score files, (if not run as root at
Zboot time).
Z
Z4.  If you would like the search daemon to be started at boot time
Zas are the other system daemons, place an entry in /etc/rc.local
Zthat looks like this:
Z
Zif [ -f /etc/searchd ]; then
Z	echo -n ' searchd'					>/dev/console
Z	/etc/searchd
Z
Z(Note that if your system clears /tmp AFTER starting searchd, the
ZAF_UNIX socket /tmp/slock will be deleted.  The default Vax 4.2
Z/etc/rc will do this!)
Z
Z5.  If you are using the "internet" feature of search, add an entry
Zto /etc/services that looks like this:
Z
Zsearch		525/tcp	searchd		# inter-machine search
Z
ZMake quite sure that this entry number (525) is not being used on
Zyour system for any other purpose (i.e. no other entries with the
Zsame number).
Z
Z
ZHow Search Works.
Z
ZThe entire i/o of the game is transmitted over stream socket lines.
ZThe screen output comes from the daemon, through the stream socket
Zto the player, and then to the screen.  The commands come from the
Zplayer to the daemon via that same stream socket.  There is one
Zconnection (i.e. one file descriptor at each end) per player in the
Zgame.  This means that the upper limit on the number of search players
Zdepends on the maximum number of files open at once.
Z
ZScore is kept by the daemon, and the score file is updated by it
Zalso.  This results in a slight delay when a player exits the
Zgame, but since the players can come from remote machines, it is
Znecessary that the daemon do the score file updates.
Z
ZShould the daemon terminate for any reason, it is possible to look
Zin a file of errors that the daemon keeps.  While many of the entries
Zin this file will not be errors, it should give some sort of reason
Zwhy it died.  This file is (on our system) "/usr/games/lib/search/errlog".
Z
ZIf it at any time becomes necessary to kill the daemon, please do
Zso with "kill -INT pid", as this will let the daemon "clean up"
Zafter itself.
Z
ZBe warned that with medium to high load averages, playing is extremely
Zpoor since screen updates come more and more slowly.  It's been my
Zexperience that search is only playable when the load is below 6
Z(on a vax 780) or below 4 (on a 750).  Failing this, you can always
Zhave some friendly super-user renice the daemon, and then have fun!
ZAlso be warned that playing search at low baud rates gives the player a
Zgreat disadvantage, since the screen output comes in faster than
Zit can be displayed.  Under the current version this will cause jerky
Zresponse for all players as the daemon's writes begin to block.  If
Zyou access a michine over a network, and then play search over the
Znetwork, all I/O will get repackaged at each step and it can get
Zhard to hit your target.  As a handicap for good players, the use
Zof several rlogin links is possible.
Z
ZMost of the '#' aliens have been removed from the game (to increase
Zperformance), but left most of the shankers '@' in (since they shoot
Zback).  For those of you that remember the old game, there is no such
Zthing as a "virgin" in this version, neither does the program keep
Ztrack of the "scab" status from game to game.
Z
ZThe score file print routine is unbelieveably primitive (compared with
Zthe original Greg Ordy cranked out).  Scores are kept by the name
Zof the player - if from a remote machine, that machine's name is also
Zincluded.
Z
ZSome code was put in the exit routines to try and keep "LAST_ACK"
Zand "FIN_WAIT_2"'s to a minimum (see the command "netstat").  Otherwise,
Zall entrances and exits from search invariably generated LAST_ACK's
Zand FIN_WAIT_2's (which look really messy).  There is a one line kernel
Zfix that gets rid of most of them, but offhand I don't remember what
Zit is (I know, big help).
Z
Z
ZPorting Search
Z
ZSearch depends heavily on the 4.2 inter-process communication scheme.
ZPorting to other operating systems will be major work.  (Volunteers?)
ZSearch uses functions with variable numbers of arguments but does *NOT*
Zuse varargs.h yet.  Since it runs on Pyramids as is, this is a low
Zpriority.  Most of the other lint has been removed.  Search also needs
Zsigned chars.  When we bump into a system which doesn't have them,
Zsomething will have to change.  Search uses temcap but not curses.
ZPadding is totally punted.  If you need to put padding in, look in
Zsearch.c.
Z
Z
ZIf you have bugs or suggestions, send them to /dev/null.
ZJust kidding.  Send them to Matt at the address below.
Z
ZDave Pare <ucbvax!sdcsvax!mr-frog> or <ihnp4!sdamos!mr-frog>
ZApril 1984
Z
ZAmended by
ZMatt Crawford	<ihnp4!oddjob!matt> or <crawford@anl-mcs.arpa> or
Z		<matt@uchicago.csnet> or <x1.crawford@uchicago.bitnet>
ZJuly/August 1985
